import ToMathlib.GroupTheory.Subgroup.Centralizer

/-!
# Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.31 — a generated subgroup is abelian iff its generators
pairwise commute

*(N&C §10.5.1, p. 455.)*

Subgroup S generated by g1..gl: all elements commute iff each pair gi,gj commutes.

## What the exercise asks, and what is proved

In the stabilizer formalism (N&C §10.5.1) one describes a subgroup `S = ⟨g₁, …, g_l⟩` of the Pauli
group `Gₙ` by a list of generators. A recurring convenience is that a property of the whole group
can be checked on the generators alone; the exercise establishes this for commutativity:

* `S` is abelian (all of its elements commute) **iff** every pair of generators commutes.

The forward direction is immediate — the generators lie in `S`, so if all elements of `S` commute
then so do the generators. The reverse direction is the substantive claim: pairwise-commuting
generators force the *entire* generated subgroup to be commutative.

Nothing in either direction uses the Pauli group, the qubit register, or any physics: it is a fact
about the subgroup **generated by an arbitrary subset of an arbitrary group** (`Subgroup.closure`).
The formalisation is in `ToMathlib/GroupTheory/Subgroup/Centralizer.lean`.

## Declarations

In `ToMathlib/GroupTheory/Subgroup/Centralizer.lean`, for a group `G` and a set `k : Set G`:

* `Subgroup.isMulCommutative_closure_iff` — Exercise 10.31: the subgroup `Subgroup.closure k`
  generated by `k` is commutative (`IsMulCommutative`) if and only if every pair of generators
  commutes, `∀ x ∈ k, ∀ y ∈ k, x * y = y * x`. Its `@[to_additive]` twin
  `AddSubgroup.isAddCommutative_closure_iff` is the additive counterpart.

The reverse implication reuses the pre-existing Mathlib lemma `Subgroup.isMulCommutative_closure`
("if all elements of `k` commute then `closure k` is commutative"); this file's addition supplies
the forward implication and packages both into the equivalence the exercise states.

## Faithfulness

* **Both directions, no weakening.** N&C's "all the elements of `S` commute" is exactly
  `IsMulCommutative (Subgroup.closure k)` — the canonical statement that the subgroup, viewed as a
  type, has commutative multiplication (equivalently, `∀ a b : closure k, a * b = b * a`). N&C's
  "`gᵢ` and `gⱼ` commute for each pair `i, j`" is `∀ x ∈ k, ∀ y ∈ k, x * y = y * x`. The theorem is
  the biconditional between the two, matching the exercise verbatim.
* **Generalisation, not restriction.** Stating the result for an arbitrary `k : Set G` in an
  arbitrary group `G`, rather than a finite generating list `g₁, …, g_l` inside the Pauli group
  `Gₙ`, is strictly more general: N&C's setting is the special case `G = Gₙ`, `k = {g₁, …, g_l}`.
  Neither direction of the argument uses finiteness of the list or anything specific to `Gₙ`.
-/
